{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>For ask now of the former days.</b> He says: it is not fitting that the Holy One, blessed be He, should permit you the sin of idolatry; rather, He must punish you for it, because no nation or tongue is obligated to the Holy One, blessed be He, as you are obligated to Him. And if you ask concerning the former days, you will find that the Holy One, blessed be He, never drew near any nation besides you — השמע עם קול אלהים וגו׳." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): every created being is obligated to worship his Creator and to praise Him, but not like Israel — this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 117:1): הללו את ה׳ כל גוים שבחוהו כל האומים — all the more so we, upon whom His kindness has prevailed. And so we find that idol worshippers are not worthy to mention the particular Name of HaShem nor to bless Him, but only Israel, as it is written (Psalms 145:21): תהלת ה׳ ידבר פי ויברך כל בשר שם קדשו — and we, who have knowledge of His particular Name, bless יה — that is, HaShem who is hidden and particular; and the reason it does not say the full Name [שלם] is that the intention is we bless Him in this world and in the world to come, which were created with יו״ד ה״א, and therefore it says מעתה ועד עולם." "<b>From the day that Elohim created man upon the earth.</b> He locked the door of inquiry: you have no permission to ask about the former days that were before you, but only from the creation of the world onward — not prior to that, for this belongs to forbidden investigation, which it is prohibited to ask or speak of, in the manner they said: what is above, what is below, what is before, what is after. And so they interpreted in tractate Ḥagigah (Job 20:4): הזאת ידעת מני עד מני שים אדם עלי ארץ — the Torah existed before the world was created, but you have no permission to inquire except מני שים אדם עלי ארץ." "<b>Has any people heard the like of it</b> is attached to השמע — that is, הנשמע כמוהו: that one heard along with [you], just as you heard ויחי — that is, that one lived, for one does not reach the point of hearing until his soul departs from him. Or one may say: ויחי — that one lives after death, as you did; and this alludes to what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: at the first utterance their souls flew away, as it is said (Song of Songs 5:6): נפשי יצאה בדברו — and the Holy One, blessed be He, brought down dew from the heavens and revived them. And so they said: Israel said, 'If we continue to hear the voice of HaShem our Elohim any more, we die' — that is, we shall surely die, as the first time; from here [we learn] that there was resurrection of the dead at the standing at Mount Sinai. And likewise the sun stood still for him [Moses] just as it stood still for Joshua; and so it was clarified in the Midrash Devarim Rabbah, and I have already mentioned this in the section of וישמע יתרו."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The verse citation (שם קמה) refers to Psalm 145, normalized to 145:21. The phrase מה שלא הזכירו שלם, 'what they did not mention complete,' refers to the truncated divine name יה instead of the full Tetragrammaton.", 2 "The citation labeled (איוב כ׳:ד׳) appears to reference Job 20:4 (הזאת ידעת מני עד…), which is the verse the Talmudic passage in Ḥagigah connects to the phrase מני שים אדם עלי ארץ; the exact attribution is preserved as written.", 3 "The phrase ויחי is cited from the continuation of the verse (Deuteronomy 5:21, וחי מי ישמע), read by Rabbeinu Bahya as 'and lived.' The allusion to Joshua refers to the sun standing still (Joshua 10:12-13); the application to Moses at Sinai is from the Midrash."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:43.138743601Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}